A||N||22||Formula88||17" and 18" rims?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||11-09-2001||03:23 PM||||Ok, I know a few people on here have run 18" rims, at least on the back of their Fiero.  In my search for rims, I've been unable to find what I'm looking for in a suitable 17" size for the rear, but I've found a possible in 18s.  <P>Konig Imagine Wheels<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Konig_Imagine_Black.jpg">  <BR>17x7" front with 215/45-17 tires<BR>18x8.5" rear with 255/35-18 tires.<P>Both sizes fit the tire mfg. specs for rim widths and they are less than 0.5% off on the speedo.  Not sure about offset, so that could be the deciding factor.  What do you think?  Will it work?  <P>Oh, the 17x7" rims are about 20lbs and the 18x8.5" rims are about 25lbs.  Is that good?  I know it's heavier than stock, but it's also a LOT bigger rim.  Pricing, depending on sales, etc., should be between $300 and $400 each for the rims.  Whaddya think? <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 11-09-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Alex4mula||11-09-2001||04:04 PM||||Those rims are sweeet   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Will go in my car very soon. I was planning exactly that same combo. I saw at Konig web that the 18x8.5 offset was 35mm. Not sure if that would work in the rear of our 88s. <BR>In TireRack a Yokohama AVS sports 255-45-18 has a diameter of 27". That's way off of your % calculation. 45 is too tall for a 18" tire. Stock 215-60-15 is about 25.2" diameter.<BR>You may be better with a 225-40-18 (25.2")or 265-35-18 (25.3") on the wide side. For the last they specify a &gt;=9" rim but I don't think 1/2" will make a big difference (I have 235-40-17 on 7" rim without problems & they spec &gt;=7.5"rim). All specs are from TireRack. Some tires the same size will have different diameters, so check specifically te brand you want. Hope this helps a little.<P><BR>PS: I thought you were selling your car...<BR>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:<p>[This message has been edited by Alex4mula (edited 11-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||11-09-2001||04:05 PM||||You might take a look at this:<P><A HREF="http://www.mmrusa.com/wheels.htm" TARGET=_blank>MMR USA</A><P>They don't have a lot of selection but the prices look good and the offsets available are the most I have seen.<P>Paul||||reg||3
Z||000003||Formula88||11-09-2001||04:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>That's way off of your % calculation. 45 is too tall for a 18" tire. Stock 215-60-15 is about 25.2" diameter.<BR>{snip}<BR>PS: I thought you were selling your car...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, that was a typo.  I meant 255/<B>35</B>-18, not 45.  That's very close the the original diameter.  And no, I'm not selling my car.  I think that's 88Formula that's selling his. (too many similar names on here!)  But I would want about a 45mm offset.  Still looking...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||11-09-2001||05:02 PM||||Sorry for the confusion. Yep, 255-35-18 would be OK. <P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||11-09-2001||07:06 PM||||I like those!!!<P>Kinda heavy (but not *too* heavy. I guess.  Especially if you go with lighter *tyres* with eg. Airmid Fibre belting.  Many people forget to calculate the *entire* rotating stock's mass.<P>You can get heavier wheels and still end up under the weight of the stock setup.<P>Offset wise, you'll want about 50mm. maybe 55mm...    or the tyre will stick out a bit.<P>With only a 35mm offset, and 8.5" wheel, you WILL have contact on the outer fenders.  Guarenteed.  <P>I am going to try and stuff 10" (yes, 10"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> wide, 18" diameter wheels in the back of my car.  However, they have an offset of 65mm, and I am using coilovers for added clearance.<P>PS: you will need coilovers too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But man, they'd be SWEEET!!<P>Maybe time for a slight (1-1.5" flare would do) 'widebody' rear?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||Formula88||11-15-2001||06:07 PM||||Ok, update.  I found on <A HREF="http://www.whalentire.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.whalentire.com</A>  that they have the Konig Imagine's in 18x7.5 and a 45mm offset.  They didn't list the 8.5" wide rim, but if it's also in a 45mm offset, that may be the way to go.  I really want to run 245s or 255s on the rear with 215 or 225s on the front.<P>Right now, I'm thinking 255/40-17 or 255/35-18 on the back and 215/45-17 or 225/45-17 on the front.<P>The tire sizes are about right to keep the staggered widths proportional, but how do you think mixing 17 and 18" rims will work?  Again, this is mainly for looks and handling traction - not drag races.  The rest of the suspension is stock with KYB shocks and struts right now, but as I do more in the future, I don't want to have to keep buying new rims and tires.  I want to find one set of Uber-tires and go with that.  I'll be keeping my stock 15" rims with stock size Bridgestone Potenza RE950's for my winter tires, and possibly shows (if I stay in stock class).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||11-15-2001||06:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>Those rims are sweeet    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Will go in my car very soon. I was planning exactly that same combo. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>BTW, are you going with the Konig's in the Black Opal or Hyper Silver?  I'm planning on the Black Opal myself.  If you use the same rims, we can't go to the same car shows, cuz our cars would be dressed alike!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jay||11-15-2001||06:40 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fast_5_rim.jpg"> <BR>These are my rims.  Fast 5 with 215/45/17 front and 225/45/17 rear with Kumho Ecxsta rubber.  Not bad and well priced.  I paid about $2000 Canadian for rims and rubber.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig2_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
